\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Maximum Likelihood Estimator (MLE)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract

\series bold
Maximum Likelihood Estimator (MLE): 
\series default
General likelihood and log likelihood funciton, regularity conditions, propertie
s of MLE, MLE of the SLM, restricted MLE and its asymptotic distribution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Maximum Likelihood Estimator (MLE)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A joint 
\series bold
probability density funciton (pdf),
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $f(x_{1},\ldots,x_{n};\theta)$
\end_inset

, of a random sample tells us the probability of a set of specific observations,
 given the 
\series bold
distribution parameters
\series default
, 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

.
 That is, a joint pdf is treated as a function of the values of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

, given 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In estimation problems, we do not know the distribution paramters, but have
 a set of observations.
 The quesiton we pose is: which distribution parameter would have generated
 the sample on hand most likely? In this estimation method, the joint pdf
 is treated as a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

, given a sample observation 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{1},\ldots,x_{n})$
\end_inset

, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $f(\theta;x_{1},\ldots,x_{n})$
\end_inset

.
 Interpreted this way, we call the joint pdf a 
\series bold
likelihood function
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The method of 
\series bold
maximum likelihood estimation
\series default
 finds the estimators of population paramaters by maximizing the likelihood
 function or the log-likelihood function with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

.
 The resulting estimator is called the 
\series bold
maximum likelihood estimator (MLE)
\series default
.
 Since the logarithmic function is a monotonically increasing function,
 maximization of the likelihood function and the log-likelihood funciton
 should yield the same solution.
 Important to note, the MLE requires knowledge of the population distribution
 funciton to specify the likelihood function while the least squares method
 does not.
 Finally, the MLE may not exist, or if it exists, it may not be unique.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To consider the MLE more formally, let 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{\prime}=(y_{1},\ldots y_{N})$
\end_inset

 be an i.i.d.
 random sample of size 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 from a population chracterized by a density 
\begin_inset Formula $f(y_{t};\theta)$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

-dimensional vector in a compact parameter space 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*
Likelihood funciton, 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

, and log-likelihood function, 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
L(y;\theta) & = & {\displaystyle \prod_{t=1}^{N}}f(y_{t};\theta)\label{eq:1}\\
l(y;\theta) & = & {\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{N}}\ln\left[f(y_{t};\theta)\right]\label{eq:2}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We also must distinguish between the global MLE and the local MLE of the
 true parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{0}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}$
\end_inset

 is the global MLE of the true parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{0}$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $l(y;\hat{\theta})\geq l(y;\theta)$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta\in\Theta$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}$
\end_inset

 is the local MLE of 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{0}$
\end_inset

 if it is a root of the gradient 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial l}{\partial\theta}=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We consider only the local MLE or consider the case of likelihood function
 which has a unique global maximum point.
 In this case, we find the MLE from the first and second order conditions
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
g(\theta) & = & \frac{\partial l(\theta)}{\partial\theta}=0\label{eq:3}\\
\mathcal{H}(\theta) & = & \frac{\partial^{2}l(\theta)}{\partial\theta\partial\theta^{\prime}}\ \ \mbox{is negative definite}\label{eq:4}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*
The MLE is a sufficient statistic and a best asymptotically normal (BAN)
 estimator.
 More importantly, the MLE has an 
\series bold
invariance property
\series default
, that is, if 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}$
\end_inset

 is the MLE of 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $h(\hat{\theta})$
\end_inset

 is the MLE of 
\begin_inset Formula $h(\theta)$
\end_inset

 for any continuous function 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Condition*
Regularity Conditions: The joint pdf 
\begin_inset Formula $L(y;\theta)$
\end_inset

 is said to be regular if
\end_layout

\begin_layout Condition*
(a) The sample space 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 for which 
\begin_inset Formula $L(y;\theta)$
\end_inset

 is strictly positive does not depend on 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

.
 (The range of 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 must not depend on 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Condition*
(b) For all 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta\in\Theta$
\end_inset

 and for all 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $L(y;\theta)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $l(y;\theta)$
\end_inset

 finite-valued partial derivatives with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 up to the third order.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Condition*
(c) The covariance matrix of 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial\ln L}{\partial\theta}$
\end_inset

 is positie definite everywhere in 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Condition*
(d) The integral 
\begin_inset Formula $\int\ln L(y;\theta)dy$
\end_inset

 is twice differentiable under the integral.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Asymptotic Properties of MLE
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The proof of the asymptotic properties of the MLE under the regularity condition
s is based on the following propositions:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
\begin_inset Formula $E\left[\frac{l(\theta_{0})}{N}\right]\geq E\left[\frac{l(\theta)}{N}\right]\ \forall\ \theta\in\Theta$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\ln(z)$
\end_inset

 is a stricly concave function, by Jensen's inequality, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left[\ln(z)\right]\leq\ln\left[E(z)\right]$
\end_inset

 for a random variable 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

.
 Take 
\begin_inset Formula $z=\frac{L(y;\theta)}{L(y;\theta_{0})}$
\end_inset

, the ratio of the likelihood functions.
 Then,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E\left[\frac{l(\theta)}{N}\right]-E\left[\frac{l(\theta_{0})}{N}\right]=E\left[\ln(z)\right]\leq\ln\left[E(z)\right]=\ln\intop\frac{L(y;\theta)}{L(y;\theta_{0})}L(y;\theta_{0})dy=\ln(1)=0
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
\begin_inset Formula $p{\displaystyle \lim_{N\rightarrow\infty}}\frac{l(\theta)}{N}=E\left[\frac{l(\theta)}{N}\right]\ \forall\ \theta\in\Theta$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset Formula $l_{t}(\theta)=\ln f(y_{t};\theta)$
\end_inset

 is an i.i.d.
 random variable.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{1}{N}l(\theta)=\frac{1}{N}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{N}}l_{t}(\theta)\equiv\bar{l}(\theta)
\]

\end_inset

Hence, by the Weak Law of Large Numbers (WLLN), the sample mean, 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{l}(\theta)$
\end_inset

, coverges to the mean 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left[l_{t}(\theta)\right]=E\left[\bar{l}(\theta)\right]$
\end_inset

 in probability.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
\begin_inset Formula $E\left[g(\theta)\right]=E\left[g_{t}\left(\theta\right)\right]=0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $Cov\left[g(\theta)\right]=N\Psi$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $Cov\left[g_{t}(\theta)\right]=\Psi$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $f(y_{t};\theta)$
\end_inset

 is a density function it must integrate to 1:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\int f(y_{t};\theta)dy_{t}=1
\]

\end_inset

Differentiation of both sides of this equation with resect to 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 gives
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\int g_{t}(\theta)f(y_{t};\theta)dy_{t}=0
\]

\end_inset

where we used the relationship 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial\ln f}{\partial\theta}=\left(\frac{1}{f}\right)\left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial\theta}\right)$
\end_inset

, which gives 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial f}{\partial\theta}=f\left(\frac{\partial\ln f}{\partial\theta}\right)$
\end_inset

.
 The left hand side is simply the definition of the expect value of 
\begin_inset Formula $g_{t}(\theta)$
\end_inset

, and hence 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left[g_{t}(\theta)\right]=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Differentiating again with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

, we can write
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\int\mathcal{H}_{t}(\theta)f(y_{t};\theta)dy_{t}+\int\left(g_{t}(\theta)g_{t}(\theta)^{\prime}\right)f(y_{t};\theta)dy_{t}=0
\]

\end_inset

The first term is 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left[\mathcal{H}_{t}(\theta)\right]$
\end_inset

, and the second term is 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left[g_{t}(\theta)g_{t}(\theta)^{\prime}\right]$
\end_inset

, which is the covariance of 
\begin_inset Formula $g_{t}(\theta)$
\end_inset

 because 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left[g_{t}(\theta)\right]=0$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Cov\left(g_{t}(\theta)\right)=E\left[g_{t}(\theta)g_{t}(\theta)^{\prime}\right]=E\left[-\mathcal{H}_{t}(\theta)\right]=\Psi
\]

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $g_{t}(\theta)$
\end_inset

 is an i.i.d.
 random variable, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E\left[g(\theta)\right] & = & {\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{N}}E\left[g_{t}(\theta)\right]=0\\
Cov\left(g(\theta)\right) & = & Cov\left({\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{N}}g_{t}(\theta)\right)={\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{N}}Cov\left(g_{t}(\theta)\right)=N\Psi
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

These results for the entire sample can also be shown by using the same
 procedure as above.
 Since the likelihood function, 
\begin_inset Formula $L(y;\theta)$
\end_inset

, is a joint density function it must integreate to 1:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\int L(y;\theta)dy=1
\]

\end_inset

Differentiation of both sides of this equation with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 gives
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\int g(\theta)L(y;\theta)dy=0
\]

\end_inset

The left hand side is simply the definition of the expect value of 
\begin_inset Formula $g(\theta)$
\end_inset

 because 
\begin_inset Formula $L(y;\theta)$
\end_inset

 is the joint density of 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left[g(\theta)\right]=0$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Differentiating again with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

, we can write
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\int\mathcal{H}(\theta)L(y;\theta)dy+\int\left(g(\theta)g(\theta)^{\prime}\right)L(y;\theta)dy=0
\]

\end_inset

The first term is 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left[\mathcal{H}(\theta)\right]$
\end_inset

, and the second term is 
\begin_inset Formula $ $
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $E\left[g(\theta)g(\theta)^{\prime}\right]$
\end_inset

, which is the covariance of 
\begin_inset Formula $g(\theta)$
\end_inset

 because 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left[g(\theta)\right]=0$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Cov\left[g(\theta)\right]=E\left[-\mathcal{H}(\theta)\right]=N\Psi
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{\sqrt{N}}g(\theta)\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{d}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array}N(0,\Psi)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset Formula $g_{t}(\theta)$
\end_inset

 is a sequence of i.i.d.
 random vectors with zero mean and covariance matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\Psi$
\end_inset

 by Proposition 3.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{1}{\sqrt{N}}g(\theta)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{N}}g_{t}(\theta)
\]

\end_inset

By the Central Limit Theorem (CLT), this converges in distribution to 
\begin_inset Formula $N(0,\Psi)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
\begin_inset Formula $p{\displaystyle \lim_{N\rightarrow\infty}\left(-\frac{1}{N}\mathcal{H}(\theta)\right)=\Psi}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \mathcal{H}_{t}(\theta)\right\} $
\end_inset

 is a sequence of i.i.d.
 random matrices.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{1}{N}\mathcal{H}(\theta)=\frac{1}{N}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{N}}\mathcal{H}_{t}(\theta)=\bar{\mathcal{H}}_{t}(\theta)
\]

\end_inset

By Khinchine's WLLN, the sample mean 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{\mathcal{H}}_{t}(\theta)$
\end_inset

 converges to its true mean 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left[\mathcal{H}_{t}(\theta)\right]=-\Psi$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}$
\end_inset

 be the MLE of the true parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{0}$
\end_inset

 for a sample of size 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

.
 Then, under the regularity conditions, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}$
\end_inset

 is consistent and has an asymptotic normal distribution, i.e.,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
p\lim_{N\rightarrow\infty}\hat{\theta} & = & \theta_{0}\\
\sqrt{N}(\hat{\theta}-\theta_{0}) & \begin{array}[b]{c}
_{d}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array} & N(0,\Psi^{-1})
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Psi=E\left(-\frac{1}{N}\mathcal{H}(\theta)\right)$
\end_inset

 is Fisher's 
\series bold
information's matrix
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof

\series bold
\shape italic
Proof of Consistency
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

From Propositions 1 and 2, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p\lim\left[\frac{l(\theta_{0})}{N}\right]=E\left[\frac{l(\theta_{0})}{N}\right]\geq E\left[\frac{l(\theta)}{N}\right]=p\lim\left[\frac{l(\theta)}{N}\right]
\]

\end_inset

for all 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

.
 In particular, the inequality holds for 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta=\hat{\theta}$
\end_inset

, and hence,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p\lim\left[\frac{l(\theta_{0})}{N}\right]\geq p\lim\left[\frac{l(\hat{\theta})}{N}\right]
\]

\end_inset

But 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}$
\end_inset

 is the MLE for every 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

, and hence,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left[\frac{l(\hat{\theta})}{N}\right]\geq\left[\frac{l(\theta)}{N}\right]
\]

\end_inset

for all 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 (including 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{0}$
\end_inset

) and for all 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

.
 Therfore,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p\lim\left[\frac{l(\hat{\theta})}{N}\right]\geq p\lim\left[\frac{l(\theta_{0})}{N}\right]
\]

\end_inset

These two inequalities imply
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p\lim\left[\frac{l(\hat{\theta})}{N}\right]=p\lim\left[\frac{l(\theta_{0})}{N}\right]
\]

\end_inset

This, in turn, implies 
\begin_inset Formula $p\lim(\hat{\theta})=\theta_{0}$
\end_inset

 when the model is asymptotically identified.
 The model is asymptotically identifed under very general conditions which
 are satisfied by the regularity conditions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof

\series bold
\emph on
Proof of Asymptotic Normality
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Take the first order Taylor expansion of 
\begin_inset Formula $g(\hat{\theta})$
\end_inset

 around the true value 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{0}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
g(\hat{\theta})=g(\theta_{0})+\mathcal{H}(\theta_{0})(\hat{\theta}-\theta_{0})
\]

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $g(\hat{\theta})=0$
\end_inset

, dividing the equation with 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{N}$
\end_inset

 and rearranging the terms we can write
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sqrt{N}(\hat{\theta}-\theta)=\left(\frac{1}{N}\mathcal{H}(\theta_{0})\right)^{-1}\frac{1}{\sqrt{N}}g(\theta_{0})
\]

\end_inset

 The first matrix on the right hand side converges in probability to 
\begin_inset Formula $\Psi$
\end_inset

 by Proposition 5, and the last term converges in distribution to 
\begin_inset Formula $N(0,\Psi)$
\end_inset

 by Proposition 4.
 Hence by using a well-known theorem, 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{N}(\hat{\theta}-\theta_{0})$
\end_inset

 converges in distribution to 
\begin_inset Formula $\Psi^{-1}Z$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $Z\sim N(0,\Psi)$
\end_inset

.
 This gives the desired result.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Alternative computational forms of the information matrix are
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\Psi & = & E\left(-\frac{1}{N}\mathcal{H}(\theta)\right)\nonumber \\
 & = & E\left(-\mathcal{H}_{t}(\theta)\right)\nonumber \\
 & = & p\mbox{lim}\left(-\frac{1}{N}\mathcal{H}(\theta)\right)\nonumber \\
 & = & E\left(-\frac{1}{N}g(\theta)g(\theta)^{\prime}\right)\nonumber \\
 & = & E\left(g_{t}(\theta)g_{t}(\theta)^{\prime}\right)\nonumber \\
 & = & p\lim\left(\frac{1}{N}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{N}}g_{t}(\theta)g_{t}(\theta)^{\prime}\right)\label{eq:5}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where subscript 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 indicates a single observation
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
g_{t}(\theta) & = & \frac{\partial\ln f(y_{t};\theta)}{\partial\theta}\label{eq:6}\\
\mathcal{H}_{t}(\theta) & = & \frac{\partial^{2}\ln f(y_{t};\theta)}{\partial\theta\partial\theta^{\prime}}=\frac{\partial g_{t}(\theta)}{\partial\theta^{\prime}}\label{eq:7}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $g(\theta)=\sum g_{t}(\theta)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}(\theta)=\sum\mathcal{H}_{t}(\theta)$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Note that the first three expressions for 
\begin_inset Formula $\Psi$
\end_inset

 require computation of the Hessian matrix, while the last three expressions
 require only the gradient vector.
 If the expectations of these forms are functions of the sample size 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

, then there limit as 
\begin_inset Formula $N\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

 is sometimes taken for 
\begin_inset Formula $\Psi$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In practice, we are interested in the MLE, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}$
\end_inset

, and its asymptotic distribution.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}$
\end_inset

 is asymptotically distributed as normal with mean 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{0}$
\end_inset

 (the true value of 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

) and covariance matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $V\equiv(N\Psi)^{-1}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Psi$
\end_inset

 is evaluated at the MLE 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}$
\end_inset

.
 The choice of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Psi$
\end_inset

 to be used in 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset

 depends primarily on the degree of difficulty in computing the expectations
 or 
\begin_inset Formula $p\lim$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

When it is difficult to compute them, their empirical values (matrices without
 taking expectaitons or 
\begin_inset Formula $p\lim$
\end_inset

) are often used for the estimate of 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
V=\mbox{asymp.}\ Cov(\hat{\theta})=\left(-\mathcal{H}(\hat{\theta})\right)^{-1}\ \mbox{or}\ \left(g(\hat{\theta})g(\hat{\theta})^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\ \mbox{or}\ \left({\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{N}g_{t}(\hat{\theta})g_{t}(\hat{\theta})}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\label{eq:8}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

In particular, the inverse of the negative Hessian matrix is used as the
 estimate of 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset

 in many software.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In some cases, it is easy to compute the expectation or 
\begin_inset Formula $p\lim$
\end_inset

 for some elements in the matrix, and not easy for other elements.
 In such cases, it is generally suggested to use the expected value of 
\begin_inset Formula $p\lim$
\end_inset

 for those elements that can be easily computed, and use the empirical values
 for other elements.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Restricted Maximum Likelihood Estimator
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $L(y;\theta)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $l(\theta)=\ln L(y;\theta)$
\end_inset

 be as defined before, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

-dimensional vector of unknown parameters.
 The gradient vector and the Hessian matrix are denoted by 
\begin_inset Formula $g(\theta)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}(\theta)$
\end_inset

, respectively.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $h(\theta)=0$
\end_inset

 be a 
\begin_inset Formula $J$
\end_inset

-dimsensional vector of restricitons on 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

.
 The restricted MLE of 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 is obtained by maximizing the Lagrange equation
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathscr{L}=l(\theta)-\lambda^{\prime}h(\theta)\label{eq:9}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $J$
\end_inset

-dimensional vector of Lagrange multipliers.
 The restricted MLE 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\theta}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\lambda}$
\end_inset

 are the solutions to the first order conditions
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
g(\tilde{\theta})-H_{\tilde{\theta}}\lambda & = & 0\label{eq:10}\\
h(\tilde{\theta}) & = & 0\label{eq:11}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{\tilde{\theta}}=\frac{\partial h(\tilde{\theta})}{\partial\theta}$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times J$
\end_inset

 matrix evaluated at 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\theta}$
\end_inset

.
 The subscript for 
\begin_inset Formula $H$
\end_inset

 emphasizes that it may be a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

